WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.000
A superhero role shot out of the sky. A&nbsp;
token tough guy this network paid to protect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.920
These are just a few stars who made big&nbsp;
bucks for a role they never even played!

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.640
It's no secret that movie stars get paid&nbsp;
an obscene amount of money for their work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.960
Between upfront deals, on-set perks, and box&nbsp;
office percentages, it's not uncommon for prolific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.920
actors to earn millions of dollars per film. It’s&nbsp;
reasonable as long as the actors are doing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.280
jobs and entertaining the masses. But sometimes&nbsp;
it transpires that an actor will receive a hefty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.640
payday and still not appear in the project they're&nbsp;
getting paid for. Sometimes their scenes simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.360
end up on the cutting room floor. Sometimes the&nbsp;
film itself doesn't come together. And sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.720
a rival studio might even bribe them to turn&nbsp;
down an offer. Whatever the case may be, here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.760
are some actors who found a way to make money from&nbsp;
roles despite not appearing in the final product.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.160
Over the course of eight years and&nbsp;
six seasons, James Gandolfini was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.920
best known to audiences worldwide as the&nbsp;
lead of HBO's crime drama "The Sopranos."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.760
One of the most acclaimed and&nbsp;
well-regarded TV shows of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.640
the saga of gangsters, their families,&nbsp;
and their therapy bills is often cited as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:16.640
originating the modern era of peak TV. A central&nbsp;
part of its success was Gandolfini as Tony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.160
a New Jersey mob boss who balanced a menacing&nbsp;
exterior with a more sensitive inner life.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.360
"Sounds like me alright."

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.480
After the show's controversial conclusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.160
any other network would have considered&nbsp;
his casting to be a massive coup.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.320
As it turns out, this nearly&nbsp;
happened with NBC and "The Office."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.200
With Steve Carell planning to depart the hit&nbsp;
comedy in its seventh season, the hunt was on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.800
to find a high profile replacement to fill his&nbsp;
shoes. Though James Spader was eventually cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.920
apparently Gandolfini was close to nabbing the&nbsp;
role. According to his "Sopranos" co-star Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:56.160
Imperioli, the actor was so close that HBO paid&nbsp;
him $3 million to turn down the role. The reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.560
given was so that Gandolfini could continue&nbsp;
developing the limited series "The Night Of"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.360
with the network. Tragically, it wouldn't&nbsp;
premiere until three years after his death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.000
It's speculated that they also wanted to&nbsp;
preserve the tough-guy legacy of Tony Soprano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.160
and were concerned that a sillier role&nbsp;
in "The Office" might detract from that.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.880
The "Harry Potter" franchise is no stranger to&nbsp;
recent controversies. Between the disappointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.440
critical and commercial reception of the&nbsp;
most recent film and author J.K. Rowling’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.000
controversial statements, the last thing these&nbsp;
spin-offs needed was one of its primary cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.720
members in hot water. Yet that's precisely&nbsp;
what happened with Johnny Depp, who had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.080
cast as the villainous Gellert Grindelwald&nbsp;
in a surprise cameo at the end of "Fantastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.560
Beasts and Where to Find Them." He later had his&nbsp;
role significantly fleshed out in the sequel.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.960
Unfortunately for Depp, personal drama&nbsp;
began to overshadow his acting career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.360
In the wake of a vicious and highly public&nbsp;
court battle with his ex-wife Amber Heard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.320
the higher-ups at Warner Bros. decided that being&nbsp;
associated with Depp was more trouble than it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.080
worth. As a result, the role of Grindelwald was&nbsp;
recast with "Hannibal" star Mads Mikkelsen for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.560
the third film. Still, Depp managed to pocket his&nbsp;
full $16 million salary per his original contract.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.212
"Contain your emotions."

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.960
With a body of work ranging from classy&nbsp;
Oscar fare to schlocky action thrillers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.320
everything in between, Nicolas Cage's career is&nbsp;
the stuff of legend. Yet one of the actor's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:27.840
infamous roles was one he never got the chance to&nbsp;
play. This of course was the title character of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:33.520
the equally legendary "Superman Lives." It was a&nbsp;
superhero blockbuster that would have been helmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:39.360
by Tim Burton with a script by Kevin Smith. It&nbsp;
seemed like a match made in comic book heaven.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.440
However, the film was not to be. Though&nbsp;
an official reason was never given,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.680
many have speculated that it may have to&nbsp;
do with the disastrous reception of Joel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.160
Schumacher's "Batman &amp; Robin." This film&nbsp;
could have made parent company Warner Bros.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:57.040
apprehensive about jumping into another&nbsp;
big-budget movie about tights and capes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.920
Jonathan Gems theorized that the studio was&nbsp;
somehow punishing the filmmaker for that film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.880
lackluster performance. The entire affair&nbsp;
was eventually chronicled in the documentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:12.080
"The Death of Superman Lives: What Happened?" And&nbsp;
though Cage never got the chance to play Kal-El,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:16.480
he did reportedly collect a cool&nbsp;
$20 million for his trouble.

